  "sections": {
    "work": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "His final print series, New Forms of 36 Ghosts (Shinkei sanjūrokuten), was \"so freakishly popular\", according to Sarah Fensom, \"that the blocks from which it was printed wore out.\"",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Yūrei-zu",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Y%C5%ABrei-zu",
          "accessed": "2026-11-16",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "His last years were among his most productive, with his great series One Hundred Aspects of the Moon (1885–1892), and New Forms of Thirty-Six Ghosts (1889–1892), as well as some masterful triptychs of kabuki theatre actors and scenes.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Tsukioka Yoshitoshi",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsukioka_Yoshitoshi",
          "accessed": "2026-11-16",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      }
    ],
    "moment": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Kuniyoshi's pupil, Yoshitoshi, has only just died, the acknowledged head of Ukioyeshi.",
        "lead": "Fenollosa, writing in 1896:",
        "source": {
          "author": "Ernest F. Fenollosa",
          "title": "The Masters of Ukioye",
          "publisher": "W. H. Ketcham, New York (Internet Archive scan)",
          "year": "1896",
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/mastersukioyeac00ketcgoog",
          "readAt": "https://archive.org/stream/mastersukioyeac00ketcgoog/mastersukioyeac00ketcgoog_djvu.txt",
          "accessed": "2026-11-16",
          "tier": 2,
          "license": "Public domain"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "But the leader of it, Yoshitoshi, a pupil of Kuniyoshi, died in 1892. He kept the old traditions for a long while ; but with that marvellous versatility which seems the strongest characteristic of the whole nation, seems to have abandoned them without a struggle.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Edward F. Strange",
          "title": "The Colour-Prints of Japan",
          "publisher": "Siegle, Hill & Co. (Internet Archive scan)",
          "year": "1904",
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/colourprintsofja00strauoft",
          "readAt": "https://archive.org/stream/colourprintsofja00strauoft/colourprintsofja00strauoft_djvu.txt",
          "accessed": "2026-11-16",
          "tier": 2,
          "license": "Public domain",
          "page": "60–62"
        }
      }
    ],
    "matters": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "… the grudges of Raigō became a giant rat and ate away at the sacred books in Enryaku-ji.",
        "lead": "The legend:",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Tesso",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tesso",
          "accessed": "2026-11-16",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "In his last years, his mental problems started to recur. In early 1891 he invited friends to a gathering of artists that did not actually exist, but rather turned out to be a delusion.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Tsukioka Yoshitoshi",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsukioka_Yoshitoshi",
          "accessed": "2026-11-16",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "He died three weeks later in a rented room, on June 9, 1892, from a cerebral hemorrhage. He was 53 years old.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Tsukioka Yoshitoshi",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
